#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:10
msgid "Improved process_iter()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:12
msgid ""
"This is part of the psutil 5.3.0 release (see the :ref:`changelog <530>` for "
"the full list of changes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:16
msgid "The old pattern"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:18
msgid ""
"Iterating over processes and collecting attributes requires more boilerplate "
"than it should. A process returned by :func:`process_iter` may disappear "
"before you access it, or require elevated privileges, so every lookup has to "
"be guarded with a ``try / except``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:23
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> for proc in psutil.process_iter():\n"
"...     try:\n"
"...         pinfo = proc.as_dict(attrs=['pid', 'name'])\n"
"...     except (psutil.NoSuchProcess, psutil.AccessDenied):\n"
"...         pass\n"
"...     else:\n"
"...         print(pinfo)\n"
"...\n"
"{'pid': 1, 'name': 'systemd'}\n"
"{'pid': 2, 'name': 'kthreadd'}\n"
"{'pid': 3, 'name': 'ksoftirqd/0'}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:38
msgid "This is not decorative. It's necessary to avoid the race condition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:41
msgid "The new pattern"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:43
msgid ""
"5.3.0 adds ``attrs`` and ``ad_value`` parameters to :func:`psutil."
"process_iter`. With these, the loop body becomes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:46
msgid ""
">>> import psutil\n"
">>> for proc in psutil.process_iter(attrs=['pid', 'name']):\n"
"...     print(proc.info)\n"
"...\n"
"{'pid': 1, 'name': 'systemd'}\n"
"{'pid': 2, 'name': 'kthreadd'}\n"
"{'pid': 3, 'name': 'ksoftirqd/0'}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:56
msgid ""
"Internally, :func:`process_iter` attach an ``info`` dict to the :class:"
"`Process` instance. The attributes are pre-fetched in one shot. Processes "
"that disappear during iteration are silently skipped, and attributes that "
"would raise :exc:`AccessDenied` gets assigned ``ad_value`` , which defaults "
"to ``None``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:62
msgid ""
"for p in psutil.process_iter(['name', 'username'], ad_value=\"N/A\"):\n"
"    print(p.name(), p.username())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:68
msgid "Performance"
msgstr "效能"

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:70
msgid ""
"Beyond the syntactic win, the new syntax is also faster than calling "
"individual methods in a loop. ``process_iter(attrs=[...])`` is equivalent to "
"using :meth:`Process.oneshot` on each process (see :doc:`One shot, twice as "
"fast </blog/2016/500-is-twice-as-fast>` for how that works): attributes that "
"share a syscall or a ``/proc`` file are fetched together instead of re-read "
"on every method call, which is a lot faster."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:78
msgid "Comprehensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:80
msgid ""
"With the exception boilerplate out of the way, comprehensions finally work "
"cleanly. E.g. getting processes owned by the current user can be written as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2017/process-iter-attrs.rst:83
msgid ""
">>> import getpass\n"
">>> from pprint import pprint as pp\n"
">>> pp([(p.pid, p.info['name']) for p in psutil.process_iter(attrs=['name', "
"'username']) if p.info['username'] == getpass.getuser()])\n"
"[(16832, 'bash'),\n"
" (19772, 'ssh'),\n"
" (20492, 'python')]"
msgstr ""
